# Acceptance benchmark config (PLAN_BROWSER_PERF_SECURITY.md D31, M34's perf-arc gate): the
# contended checkout endpoint from testFlow-tests' own `perf-0` milestone
# (testFlow-tests/PLAN_WEBV2_TARGETS.md §2) — a single hot product whose row every VU races via
# `OrdersService.persistOrderAtomically`'s conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE stock >= :qty`. Needs
# testFlow-tests' Docker stack running (`node cli.mjs start` in that repo) and its load target
# reset (`POST /admin/load/reset`, bearer admin auth) before a clean run.

# tflw `M239` `D` (`D1319`): `generator-saturation-demo.tflw` loads `./helpers.ts` from this
# directory, which is neither of the defaults (`./helpers`, `./tests/helpers`) — so the corpus
# root itself is the one allowed directory, written out rather than moving a one-file helper into
# a folder of its own. Without this line the corpus is `TF083` under tflw ≥ M239 and
# `check:acceptance` goes red (found on the first CI run after tflw#238 merged).
helpers "."

env local default
  api "http://localhost:4001/v1"

require env LOAD_USER_EMAIL, LOAD_USER_PW

# Bearer auth, not `session-login`'s cookie form — matches testFlow-tests' own `tflw.config`
# convention for the (mutating-request) `admin` session: cookie sessions there enforce a
# double-submit CSRF header (`session-auth.guard.ts`) that a bearer token sidesteps entirely,
# and there is no reason to fight that for a load-generation identity that only ever calls two
# fixed endpoints.
session load
  api POST /auth/login body { email: env(LOAD_USER_EMAIL), password: env(LOAD_USER_PW) }
  expect status equals 200
  capture body.accessToken as token
  header "Authorization" is "Bearer {token}"
